<Base title="The Common's first weeks | Our story" description="Peter's first update to The Common members in November 2017, and the work of turning a Squamish workspace into a community.">
  <header class="page-hd"><div class="wrap"><h1>Our first few weeks.</h1><p>In November 2017, Peter wrote to the founding members about what was working, what was not, and what he wanted The Common to become.</p></div></header>
  <article class="section light"><div class="wrap story-body">
    <p class="story-date">Based on Peter’s November 9, 2017 update</p>
    <h2>Getting the people together.</h2>
    <p>The Common had only been open a few weeks. Peter and the team had made a warm place to work inside an original 1970s Squamish building. Founding members had taken a chance on it. In his first update, Peter was honest about the part that still needed attention: people were coming in to work, but he had not done enough to connect them.</p>
    <blockquote>“Our goal was to build community.”</blockquote>
    <p>He wanted that community to include both members and people outside the space. He promised a calendar of events, invited everyone down for a Friday social, and asked them to tell him what could be better. The first drink was on The Common.</p>
    <h2>What members asked for.</h2>
    <p>That first note was practical too. Peter listed what the team had just worked on: better Wi-Fi, more power sockets, warmer lighting, changes to the meeting rooms, and a fridge. He also laid out what was next, including sound insulation, cleaner air, and an outdoor garden where people could meet.</p>
    <p>The early membership included a local partner discount card and an event token for a drink or entry to a networking event. Those were 2017 offers, not current membership benefits. What matters now is why they were there: Peter wanted it to be easier for members to meet each other and take part in Squamish beyond their own desk.</p>
    <h2>Still the idea.</h2>
    <p>The Common has changed since those first weeks. The rooms and the ways people book them have changed too. Peter's point in that first note has stayed clear: a good workspace should help people do their own work and find their people here.</p>
    <p class="story-back"><a class="inline-link" href="/community/">Back to the community</a></p>
  </div></article>
</Base>
